  "About": "",
  "About GpxAnalyzer": "",
  "GpxAnalyzer is a professional Android application for GPS track analysis, built as a 19-module clean architecture project. It processes GPX, KML, TCX, FIT, GeoJSON, KMZ, and CSV files through a multi-stage pipeline — from FFT-based wavelet noise reduction through extrema detection to synchronized multi-chart and map visualization, offline terrain tile elevation, spatial heatmap overlays, and format conversion with full field compatibility awareness — all running entirely on your device.": "",
  "Signal Processing Pipeline": "",
  "Raw GPS data enters a wavelet-based noise reduction stage using adaptive window sizing computed via FFT frequency domain analysis. Multiple window functions (Triangular, Hanning, Gaussian) are applied based on signal characteristics. A multi-stage derivative analysis with amplitude thresholding then detects extrema — peaks, valleys, and trend segments — even across irregular sampling intervals. Activity-specific configurations (Alpine Skiing, Trail Running) adjust detection parameters automatically based on the selected activity type.": "",
  "Dual-Domain Visualization": "",
  "The chart system supports both time-based and distance-based domains, each with isolated cumulative statistics to prevent cross-domain data contamination. Three view modes (Single, Split, Multi) display altitude, speed, trend boundaries, and scatter plots. A settings dialog exposes 8 adjustable segment detection parameters with per-domain and per-activity-type persistence. All charts synchronize selections bidirectionally with the map — tap a point on any chart and the map pans to that location, or tap the map and all charts highlight the nearest trackpoint.": "",
  "Route Drawing &amp; Elevation Profiles": "",
  "Drawing mode supports both tap-to-place and freehand input — draw routes by dragging across the map with automatic stroke simplification. Catmull-Rom spline smoothing turns sharp vertex-to-vertex segments into smooth curves with adjustable tension and detail. The Drawing Tools dialog provides unified controls for curve smoothing, freehand simplification, and elevation sampling step, each with contextual help. A live distance pill shows total route length, and a mini elevation chart overlays the map. Drawn routes can be joined to a loaded GPX track and saved as one file.": "",
  "A grid-based spatial visualization layer colors geographic cells by point density or mean elevation. The grid adapts to zoom level with stable rendering during pan and zoom. Four overlay control buttons toggle the heatmap, data point markers, density/elevation metric, and a 4-state source filter (All Sources, API, GPX Track, Terrain Tile). Elevation data is fetched offline from AWS Terrain Tiles at ~30m SRTM resolution, decoded from RGB pixel channels on-device, and stored in a three-layer cache (in-memory LRU → Room database → cloud). A multi-zoom tile fallback cascade (z15 → z14 → z13) fills satellite data voids with zero API fallbacks needed. Tile boundary overlays visualize cached coverage, blind spot detection highlights gaps with red polygon warnings, and background prefetch via WorkManager expands coverage as you explore. Per-zoom cache management shows tile counts by zoom level with selective clearing and compression.": "",
  "Multi-Format File Conversion": "",
  "GpxAnalyzer opens GPX, KML, KMZ, TCX, FIT, GeoJSON, and CSV files — select multiple files at once or tap any track file to open it directly via Android's built-in \"Open with\" menu. Convert between formats with automatic field mapping for heart rate, cadence, power, temperature, and 18+ data fields. A visual compatibility matrix shows exactly which fields each format supports before you convert, and a data loss diagram highlights what gets dropped — no surprises on export. Format and activity type filtering with persistent preferences keeps your file list organized.": "",
  "Interactive Mapping Engine": "",
  "Built on OSMDroid, the map engine renders gradient color-coded polylines using a two-layer border+fill technique with O(n) color computation off the main thread. Map rotation uses two-finger gestures with a tappable compass overlay. Nearest-point detection runs Haversine distance calculations within a 30dp screen-space threshold. Animated pulsating boundary markers display geographic bearing arrows computed from adjacent trackpoint coordinates.": "",
  "Why Strava Sync Was Removed": "",
  "GpxAnalyzer previously offered Strava sync via OAuth 2.0 with cache-aware activity downloads. Strava has since introduced fees and a paid subscription requirement for developers to access its API. GpxAnalyzer is, and will remain, completely free — no ads, no subscriptions, no in-app purchases — so Strava sync has been removed rather than pass those costs on to users. Every other import, conversion, and analysis feature is unaffected.": "",
  "Architecture": "",
  "A 19-module Gradle project with dependency injection, reactive pipelines, and strict layer separation. The architecture is organized into foundation, feature, and customized library layers — each with clearly defined responsibilities and boundaries. L1/L2/L3 multi-level caching with per-slot and domain isolation, reactive state management, and lifecycle-aware subscription handling ensure smooth performance across the entire app.": "",
  "All GPX processing happens locally. Minimum version enforcement ensures users always run a supported release. A mandatory privacy policy acceptance flow runs at startup with update detection and offline caching. GDPR-compliant with zero data collection or tracking.": "",
  "Frequently Asked Questions": "",
  "Quick answers about GPX analysis, supported formats, privacy, and offline use": "",
  "What is GpxAnalyzer?": "",
  "GpxAnalyzer is a free Android app for analyzing recorded GPS tracks from hiking, running, cycling, skiing and other outdoor activities. It opens GPX and other track files, removes GPS noise, and shows elevation, speed and gradient charts synchronized with an interactive map, all processed on your device.": "",
  "Which GPS file formats can I open, and can I convert GPX to KML, TCX, FIT or GeoJSON?": "",
  "You can import GPX, TCX, KML, KMZ, FIT, GeoJSON and CSV files, open them straight from other apps with Android's \"Open with\" menu, and convert between formats. A compatibility matrix shows which fields such as heart rate, cadence, power and temperature each format keeps before you export.": "",
  "Does GpxAnalyzer work offline?": "",
  "Yes. All track processing, charts and statistics run on your device without a connection. Map imagery and terrain elevation tiles are downloaded the first time you view an area and cached, so previously viewed areas keep working offline.": "",
  "Is GpxAnalyzer free? Does it have ads or subscriptions?": "",
  "GpxAnalyzer is completely free on Google Play with no ads, no subscriptions and no in-app purchases. If you find it useful, you can support development with an optional donation.": "",
  "Does the app track my location or collect personal data?": "",
  "No. GpxAnalyzer does not request location permissions and never tracks where you are. It only reads the track files you choose to open, and nothing is uploaded or shared. There is no analytics or advertising SDK, and the app is GDPR-compliant. See the <a href=\"privacy_policy.html\">privacy policy</a> for details.": "",
  "How does the GPS noise reduction work?": "",
  "Raw GPS points are cleaned with wavelet-based signal processing that adapts its smoothing window to each track using frequency analysis. This removes roughly 85 to 95 percent of GPS jitter while preserving real peaks, valleys and climb or descent segments. Presets for activities such as trail running and alpine skiing tune the detection automatically.": "",
  "Can I draw my own route and see its elevation profile?": "",
  "Yes. Tap to place points or draw freehand on the map, smooth the line with adjustable curve settings, and watch the distance and a live elevation profile update as you draw. A drawn route can be joined to an existing track and saved as a single GPX file.": "",
  "Does GpxAnalyzer sync with Strava?": "",
  "Strava sync was removed because Strava now charges developers for API access, and GpxAnalyzer is committed to staying free. You can export any Strava activity as a GPX file and open it in GpxAnalyzer, and every import, conversion and analysis feature still works.": "",
  "Which Android versions does GpxAnalyzer support?": "",
  "GpxAnalyzer runs on Android 9 or newer, on both phones and tablets, and uses edge-to-edge display on Android 15 and later. It is available on Google Play.": "",
  "How do I open a GPX file on Android, and what app do I need?": "",
  "Install GpxAnalyzer from Google Play, then open the .gpx file from your file manager, email or messaging app using Android's \"Open with\" menu and pick GpxAnalyzer — or import it from inside the app. The track appears on the map with synchronized elevation and speed charts. GPX exports from GPS watches, phones and web services all work, as do TCX, KML, KMZ, FIT, GeoJSON and CSV files.": "",
  "Does GpxAnalyzer show the elevation profile of a GPX track?": "",
  "Yes. Every track gets an elevation profile chart synchronized with the map, alongside speed and gradient charts and per-track statistics. Tapping a point on a chart highlights the same spot on the map. When a recording has no usable altitude data, elevation can be taken from offline terrain tiles instead.": "",
